# Watching Nested Objects

By default, watchers don't detect changes inside nested objects. To watch deeply, set the `deep` option.

```javascript
watch: {
  user: {
    handler(newVal, oldVal) {
      console.log('User changed:', newVal);
    },
    deep: true
  }
}
```

This will trigger whenever any property inside `user` changes (even nested).

⚠️ **Performance note**: Deep watchers can be expensive. Use them sparingly.

# Run Watcher Immediately

Sometimes you want the watcher to run **right away** when the component is created, not just after the first change.

```javascript
watch: {
  searchTerm: {
    handler(newVal) {
      this.fetchResults(newVal);
    },
    immediate: true
  }
}
```

With `immediate: true`, the handler runs once during component creation.

If you need to watch multiple reactive sources and run a single handler, use a **computed property** that combines them, then watch that computed property.

```javascript
computed: {
  searchCriteria() {
    return { query: this.search, category: this.category };
  }
},
watch: {
  searchCriteria: {
    handler() { this.fetch(); },
    deep: true
  }
}
```
